@prefix ag:   <http://example.org/agora#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix xsd:  <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

<http://example.org/agora/simulated-actuation> a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "Agora simulated actuation" ;
    rdfs:comment """Redeeming a voucher against a device that does not exist.

A separate capability with a separate vocabulary, not the real one disarmed. An agent holds ag:hasActuator or ag:hasSimulatedActuator, never a device whose behaviour depends on a flag — so a world cannot claim to hold a valve and then quietly refuse to open it, and you can tell which it is by reading the world.

There is no dry-run mode in the production package. That is what this package is for.""" .

#################  The capability  #################
# A KIND of actuation, so whoever needs "something that can redeem" finds it — the host asks
# for ag:Actuation and gets whichever of the two this agent derived, exactly as a bidder asks
# for perception and gets whichever kind it has.

ag:SimulatedActuation rdfs:subClassOf ag:Actuation ; rdfs:label "Simulated actuation" ;
    rdfs:comment "The dose is computed, bounded by the device's own calibration, spent once, and announced — everything the real capability does except convince hardware. No signature, because there is nothing to convince." .

#################  What it is wired to  #################

ag:SimulatedActuator a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf ag:Device ; rdfs:label "Simulated actuator" ;
    rdfs:comment "A device that is not there. Deliberately NOT an ag:Actuator: the production derivation matches actuators, so a simulated one can never grant the capability that moves water." .

ag:SimulatedValve a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf ag:SimulatedActuator ; rdfs:label "Simulated valve" .

ag:hasSimulatedActuator a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Agent ; rdfs:range ag:SimulatedActuator ;
    rdfs:label "has simulated actuator" ;
    rdfs:comment "The counterpart of ag:hasActuator, and a different property on purpose: wiring meant for hardware cannot accidentally grant a simulation, or the reverse." .
